# G041 — Country from IP Lookup

*v2. See `ip-lookup.md` for prose: identity-to-context resolution,
private addresses as unresolvable, binary search over sorted ranges
as dispatch table, proximity routing connects geography to
choreography.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Jurisdiction-Aware Operations

[compliance-check @visitor-ip
    IN ORDER TO
        resolve the visitor's jurisdiction from their IP
        route to the correct compliance regime per region
        and pass only if the rule-engine approves the operation

    @geo <- [geo[lookup {ip: @visitor-ip}]]

    @geo:country-code
        where is "EU" or "GB"  -> @rules <- [compliance[gdpr-check]]
        where is "US"          -> @rules <- [compliance[ccpa-check]]
        where is "private"      -> -> #internal-traffic (skip)
        otherwise               -> @rules <- [compliance[default-check]]

    @rules
        where passes all gates    -> [process {visitor-ip: @visitor-ip}]
        otherwise                  -> #compliance-failed
]

---

## Verb Surface

[geo
    lookup              {ip}                    ;; -> Record | "private"
    country-breakdown   {ips}                    ;; -> {country-code -> count}
    nearest-server      {client-ip, server-ips}  ;; -> server-ip
    great-circle-km     {lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: private ranges as unresolvable `where`

[geo[lookup {ip: @ip}]
    IN ORDER TO
        recognise RFC 1918 / loopback / link-local as unresolvable
        because private IPs exist only within their local network
        and guessing a country for them is worse than saying "private"

    @ip
        where in 10.0.0.0/8
           or in 172.16.0.0/12
           or in 192.168.0.0/16
           or in 127.0.0.0/8
           or in 169.254.0.0/16
            -> "private"
        otherwise
            -> binary-search @geo-ranges for range containing @ip
               return record with country, city, lat, lon
]

Some `@references` are similarly unresolvable outside their scope —
a local variable is valid but has no global meaning. The resolver's
scope boundary is the same shape.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: binary search `where` ranges are pre-sorted

[geo[lookup]
    @geo-ranges sorted by start-ip ascending
    binary-search for range where start-ip at most @ip at most end-ip
    O(log n) rather than O(n)
]

Same dispatch shape InnateScript's resolver uses for namespaced
references: sorted ranges + binary search = fast lookup. G013's
credit card prefix dispatch used linear scan; G041 commits to the
logarithmic variant.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [geo[lookup {ip: "8.8.8.8"}]]
    ;; -> {country: "United States", code: "US",
    ;;     city: "Mountain View", lat: 37.4, lon: -122.1}

    -> [geo[lookup {ip: "192.168.1.1"}]]
    ;; -> "private"

    -> [geo[nearest-server
              {client-ip: "8.8.8.8",
               server-ips: ["1.1.1.1", "9.9.9.9", "208.67.222.222"]}]]
    ;; -> whichever is geographically closest
]
